ually the server may close the connection if we don't handle keep-alive. HTTP/1.1 default keep-alive. Let me handle reconnection on failure.\n\nLet me rewrite with http.client keep-alive, keep CONCURRENCY moderate (say 16) and see throughput. But I want to be a bit careful climbing. Let me do 16 threads with keep-alive.\n\nActually the previous jump 2→8 gave throughput 2.6→5.9 which suggests client bottleneck around there. With keep-alive, per-connection throughput goes up a lot because we skip handshake. Let me try 16 threads with keep-alive, duration 12.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: If keep-alive makes each thread pull much more, 16 threads could massively exceed client capacity and just thrash. But we want to stress the SERVER. Even if client can't consume at full rate, the act of many concurrent requests stresses the server's connection handling and CPU. Let me just try.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the real lever for \"RTT + loss on target\" is probably server CPU/network-stack saturation. More concurrent connections and connection churn (new connections per request) stress the server's accept queue and TCP state more than keep-alive. But more bytes/sec stresses the NIC/driver.\n\nGiven RTT rose with more threads (connection churn), maybe connection churn is the lever. urllib opens a new connection each time = high churn. 8 threads → lots of connections/sec (11512 downloads/12s ≈ 960 conn/s).\n\nSo to increase RTT further, maybe increase connection churn (more threads, new connection per request). Let me increase to 32 threads. But client Python GIL may cap.\n\nLet me use multiprocessing + threads to bypass GIL: e.g., 4 processes × 8 threads = 32 concurrent workers, each creating a new connection per request (urllib). This maximizes both parallelism and connection churn without GIL bottleneck.\n\nThat's a solid plan for round 3. Let me write it with multiprocessing.\n\nActually, careful: multiprocessing + urllib, each process independent. Let me do procs=